(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What should a patient with a pyogenic granuloma be told?","choices":["It is benign and often resolves over months to years; treatment is available if wanted","The lesion is premalignant and must be excised urgently to prevent transformation","The lesion is infectious and household contacts should be examined","The lesion will fade within a year and no follow-up is necessary"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — treatments exist for cosmetic reasons or to prevent bleeding. If it recurs, early follow-up is appropriate, because small lesions are easier to treat than large ones. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man's family are alarmed by dozens of dark warty growths that appeared on his back over two years. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["They are a sign of an underlying internal malignancy and warrant urgent systemic investigation","They are contagious growths spread by contact and will need isolation until they are treated","They are benign, common in older adults, and the diagnosis is clinical without any need for biopsy","They are precancerous and each one carries a measurable chance of becoming a squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — cryotherapy helps if a lesion becomes itchy or inflamed, though it may recur. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with suspected Rocky Mountain spotted fever is on day 4 of illness. The indirect immunofluorescence assay has been sent. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start doxycycline now, since treatment should begin by day 5 and the assay is rarely diagnostic before day 7","Wait for the assay result, since the gold standard test should guide the choice of antibiotic","Start amoxicillin now, since doxycycline should be reserved for confirmed disease","Repeat the assay in three days and treat only if it has become positive by then"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — treatment must not wait for the serology to return. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has recurrent scaling of the scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds and external ears. Examination shows greasy, yellow-white scale over mildly erythematous poorly demarcated patches. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["High-potency topical corticosteroids as monotherapy","Topical mupirocin","Ketoconazole shampoo and topical ketoconazole cream","Oral terbinafine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Topical antifungals are the mainstay because Malassezia overgrowth drives the condition; steroids may be added early to reduce inflammation. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man who keeps a pet tarantula has an itchy papular rash on his forearms and a red, gritty eye. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Oral antihistamine for the skin, with artificial tears and review in two weeks","Oral ivermectin for the skin, with topical antibiotic drops for the affected eye","Surgical removal of each embedded hair from the skin and from the eye surface","Topical corticosteroid for the skin, with ophthalmology referral for the eye"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — shed hairs embed in skin and eyes, causing conjunctivitis and corneal granuloma. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has a suspected serpentine skin lesion but the diagnosis is uncertain and follicular pustules are also present. Which test is most appropriate?","choices":["Dermoscopy of the lesion looking for the ovoid eggs within the affected skin","Bacterial culture of an unroofed pustule sent for Gram stain and sensitivities","Light microscopy with mineral oil, which shows live and dead larvae in folliculitis","Serology for immunoglobulin M and immunoglobulin G against the responsible organism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the serpentine rash alone is enough for a clinical diagnosis. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man started lamotrigine three weeks ago. He has three days of fever and sore throat, then painful dusky macules on the trunk, erosions of the lips and conjunctivae, and epidermal detachment over about 6% of his body surface. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome","Erythema multiforme major","Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Toxic epidermal necrolysis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — detachment under 10% with mucosal involvement, three weeks into an aromatic anticonvulsant. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has a rapidly enlarging pink nodule on the shoulder with no pigment at all. It has been present six weeks. Which melanoma subtype should you consider, and why is it dangerous?","choices":["Acral lentiginous melanoma — it grows rapidly and is often amelanotic on the palms and soles","Nodular melanoma — it grows rapidly, is often amelanotic, and may lack the classic recognition features","Superficial spreading melanoma — it grows rapidly, is often amelanotic, and may lack the classic features","Lentigo maligna melanoma — it grows rapidly on chronically sun-exposed skin of older adults"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the absence of pigment and of the mnemonic features delays diagnosis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is a positive Nikolsky sign?","choices":["Lesions develop at sites of trauma","Fine white lacy lines appearing across the surface of the affected plaques","The top layers of skin slip away or slough off from the lower layers when rubbed","Removal of scale produces pinpoint bleeding"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — positive in pemphigus, negative in bullous pemphigoid. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 130"},{"q":"Describe the clinical appearance of lentigines and where they occur.","choices":["Small light brown symmetric macules 3 to 5 mm across, appearing on sun-exposed skin in fair individuals","Beige to brown to black papules that feel velvety and look stuck onto the surface of the skin","Well-circumscribed round to oval uniformly black or brown macules under 5 mm, on skin, conjunctiva and mucocutaneous surfaces","Well-defined macules with irregular borders that coalesce at sites of severe sunburn, from under 1 mm to several centimetres"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they occur on both sun-exposed and sun-protected areas. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A patient with folliculitis has not responded to a topical antibiotic and the infection is now extensive. Which oral agents apply?","choices":["Erythromycin or clarithromycin","Doxycycline or minocycline","Cephalexin or dicloxacillin","Penicillin V or amoxicillin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, ciprofloxacin or linezolid if resistance is suspected. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man with pityriasis rosea asks what to expect. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["It is self-limited and typically fades over 4 to 6 weeks, though darker skin may show post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation for several months","It requires a six-week course of oral antibiotics","It will persist indefinitely without phototherapy","It is highly contagious by direct skin contact, so he should avoid close contact with others until every last lesion has faded"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — reassurance is the mainstay, with antihistamines or topical steroids used cautiously for itch. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 171"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has a mildly inflamed eczematous patch on the eyelid and another on the vulva. Which topical corticosteroid is most appropriate?","choices":["Betamethasone dipropionate 0.05% twice daily","Triamcinolone acetonide 0.1% twice daily","Hydrocortisone, or another low potency agent","Clobetasol propionate 0.05%, for a short course"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — potency is chosen by site, and these are the thinnest skin in the body. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which pairing correctly separates pretest from posttest probability?","choices":["Pretest is estimated from signs, symptoms, history and frequency; posttest additionally reflects the test result","Pretest probability applies to screening programmes only, while posttest probability applies to diagnostic testing","Pretest uses sensitivity and specificity; posttest uses risk factors","Both are determined solely by how common the disease is"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This matches the lecture's two definitions and their listed inputs. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A healthy 55-year-old asks about the shingles vaccine. What should she be offered?","choices":["No vaccine, since she is under 65 and immunocompetent","Two doses of recombinant zoster vaccine, at a standard interval of 2 to 6 months","One dose of recombinant zoster vaccine, with no second dose required","Two doses of recombinant zoster vaccine, but only from the age of 65"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — two doses for immunocompetent adults 50 and over. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man of Mediterranean origin has slowly progressive purple plaques on both lower legs over several years. He is not immunosuppressed. Which form of Kaposi sarcoma is this?","choices":["Iatrogenic — occurring with immunosuppressive therapy and improving when doses are reduced","Epidemic — occurring in acquired immunodeficiency and treated first with antiretroviral therapy","Classic — older men, chronic course, rarely fatal, related to age-associated immune senescence","Endemic — often aggressive in young Black men in equatorial Africa and sometimes rapidly fatal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the chronic indolent form. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 29-year-old fair-skinned woman has a 4 mm naevus on the shoulder that is very dark brown, almost black, unlike her other moles. Which is the most appropriate interpretation?","choices":["Dark colour indicates a seborrheic keratosis, which is benign and needs no action","Very dark brown or black on light skin is suspicious and warrants further assessment","Dark colour is expected in a common acquired naevus and needs no further attention","Dark colour indicates a blue naevus, which is benign and needs only observation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — common acquired naevi are usually skin coloured, brown or pink with homogenous colour. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which rosacea complication requires urgent ophthalmology referral?","choices":["Papules and pustules spreading beyond the central face","Ocular rosacea with keratitis, visual symptoms or corneal involvement","Rhinophyma causing nasal airway narrowing over several years","Persistent central facial erythema unresponsive to topical therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — untreated ocular disease risks corneal scarring. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"How does a callus differ from a corn?","choices":["It comes from repeated scratching, is lichenified with exaggerated skin markings, is plaque-like, and itches intensely","It comes from broad-area pressure, is diffusely thickened without a central core, is larger and irregular, and is usually painless","It comes from focal pressure, has a cone-shaped keratin core, is smaller and well defined, and hurts on direct pressure","It comes from viral infection, has a blackened centre, interrupts the skin lines, and hurts on side pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — no core, bigger, vaguer, and it does not hurt. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Why must malignancy and infection be excluded in a rapidly expanding ulcer before treating it as pyoderma gangrenosum?","choices":["Because the diagnosis is one of exclusion and the immunosuppression used would worsen an infection","Because pyoderma gangrenosum is itself a premalignant condition","Because antibiotics would become the first-line treatment once an infection is confirmed","Because a biopsy cannot be taken once immunosuppression has started"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the question to answer is why this ulcer is suddenly expanding. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A colleague suggests incising and draining the swollen fingertip of a patient with herpetic whitlow. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Do not incise and drain it, because that does not treat the virus and can delay healing","Incise and drain it, because decompression shortens the course of the infection","Incise and drain it, but only after 72 hours of oral antiviral therapy have failed","Do not incise and drain it, because the lesion is sterile and will resolve within 24 hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — explicitly contraindicated. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"What causes a pressure injury?","choices":["Unrelieved pressure damaging underlying tissue, generally soft tissue compressed between a bony prominence and an external surface for a prolonged time","Repeated friction over a broad area of skin, producing diffuse hyperkeratosis with a poorly defined margin and no central keratin core at all","Disruption of the skin over the coccyx drawing in hair and debris, with follicular plugging that prevents drainage","Loss of the control mechanisms that normally regulate tissue repair, producing overgrowth of dense fibrous tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the injury ranges from intact skin to ulcers reaching bone. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A patient with pyoderma gangrenosum asks why the wound clinic will not debride the ulcer as they would for other wounds. Which is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["The ulcer is infected and debridement would spread bacteria into the bloodstream","The ulcer will heal faster if left completely undisturbed by any dressing","Debridement is deferred only until systemic immunosuppression has taken effect","The ulcer worsens with trauma, a phenomenon called pathergy, so debridement makes it larger"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — this is the single most consequential management point in the condition. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"How does an acute pilonidal abscess present, and how does chronic disease present?","choices":["Acutely, non-blanchable erythema over intact skin; chronically, a deep ulcer extending as far as bone","Acutely, sudden pain and swelling in or along the gluteal cleft; chronically, recurrent drainage and pain from one or more sinus tracts","Acutely, recurrent purulent drainage from several sinus tracts; chronically, sudden pain and swelling over the sacrum","Acutely, a rapidly growing vascular nodule that bleeds easily; chronically, a flat uniformly pigmented patch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a hair may occasionally be seen protruding from a sinus opening. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 66-year-old has a reddish, shiny, scaly thin plaque on the upper back with a thready pearly border and spotty pigment at the edge. Which subtype is this?","choices":["Morpheaform basal cell carcinoma","Pigmented basal cell carcinoma","Superficial basal cell carcinoma","Nodular basal cell carcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — back and chest are its typical sites. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 44-year-old shows you a mole on her upper back. One half does not match the other, the edge is notched, and there are brown, black and pink areas within it. It is 8 mm across and she says it has changed this year. Which recognition criteria does this lesion meet?","choices":["Asymmetry, border irregularity and colour variegation only, since 8 mm is below the diameter threshold","Border irregularity and evolution only, since colour variation within a lesion is a benign feature","Diameter and evolution only, since asymmetry and border irregularity are not part of the mnemonic","Asymmetry, border irregularity, colour variegation, diameter over 6 mm, and evolution — all five"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — every element of the mnemonic is present. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which three lice cause pediculosis, and which body regions do they affect?","choices":["Phthirus pubis on the scalp, Pediculus humanus capitis on pubic skin, and Cimex pilosellus on the trunk","Pediculus humanus capitis on the scalp, Pediculus humanus humanus on the trunk, Phthirus pubis on pubic skin","Pediculus humanus capitis on the trunk, Pediculus humanus humanus on the scalp, Phthirus pubis on the eyelashes","Sarcoptes scabiei on the scalp, Cimex lectularius on the trunk, and Phthirus pubis on pubic skin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The body louse is about 30% larger than the head louse. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A patient has pityriasis versicolor over the upper trunk. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Oral therapy, such as fluconazole or itraconazole, given to every patient at presentation whatever the extent of disease","Oral terbinafine, given for six weeks because it concentrates well in sweat","Topical nystatin, applied twice daily until the scale has fully resolved","Topical therapy, such as ketoconazole shampoo or cream, selenium sulfide, zinc pyrithione, ciclopirox, or topical terbinafine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — topical therapy is first-line. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman stung by a wasp develops generalised urticaria, lip swelling and wheeze within ten minutes. Which is the most appropriate immediate treatment?","choices":["Calcium gluconate with a muscle relaxant and a benzodiazepine given intravenously","Epinephrine, given intramuscularly or subcutaneously, with emergency transfer and supportive measures","An oral antihistamine with a short course of prednisolone and review the following day","Ice to the sting site with injection of a local anaesthetic for control of the pain"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — this is an anaphylactic reaction rather than a severe local one. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which comorbidities are patients with psoriasis at increased risk of?","choices":["Asthma and allergic rhinitis","Cardiovascular events, type 2 diabetes mellitus, metabolic syndrome and lymphoma","Chronic kidney disease, hepatic cirrhosis and early-onset osteoporosis","Osteoporosis and vitamin D deficiency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — psoriasis is a systemic inflammatory disease, not only a skin condition. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 155"},{"q":"Which single feature most usefully distinguishes erythema nodosum from nodular vasculitis?","choices":["Erythema nodosum nodules occur exclusively in men","Erythema nodosum nodules do not ulcerate","Erythema nodosum nodules appear only on the posterior legs","Erythema nodosum nodules are painless on palpation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and they sit on the anterior shins whereas nodular vasculitis favours the posterior legs and ulcerates. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What are the two primary clinical priorities in stasis dermatitis?","choices":["Distinguishing it from cellulitis, and determining whether compression therapy can be used safely","Identifying the causative organism by culture and selecting an appropriate antibiotic","Determining the allergen and arranging patch testing","Staging the ulcer and measuring its depth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both are named as the priorities. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 39-year-old has a 4 mm pigmented lesion that is asymmetric with an irregular border and has darkened over three months. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure and review in a year, because a lesion under 6 mm cannot be melanoma","Photograph and review in six months, because evolution over three months is too rapid for melanoma","Refer for sentinel lymph node biopsy before sampling the lesion","Biopsy or excise it, because smaller lesions can still be melanoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the 6 mm figure is a prompt, not a rule-out. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which shaving advice is given for pseudofolliculitis barbae?","choices":["Continue the current routine and treat each papule with a topical antibiotic","Stop shaving if possible, use clean razors, and avoid lift-and-cut razor systems","Shave daily against the grain with a multi-blade razor to cut hairs below the surface","Shave weekly with an electric razor set to the closest possible cutting length"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — mild razor angles and single or at most double blades are preferred. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which two combination topical products are named for reducing antibiotic resistance in acne?","choices":["Differin (adapalene with benzoyl peroxide) and Tazorac (tazarotene with benzoyl peroxide)","Cleocin T (clindamycin with salicylic acid) and Erygel (erythromycin with salicylic acid)","Altabax (retapamulin with mupirocin) and Bactroban (mupirocin with benzoyl peroxide)","BenzaClin (clindamycin with benzoyl peroxide) and Benzamycin (erythromycin with benzoyl peroxide)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both pair a topical antibiotic with benzoyl peroxide. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"You are counting café au lait macules on that same 9-year-old. How many are described as diagnostic, and what caveat applies?","choices":["Six or more; and the macules alone establish the diagnosis","Three or more; but only if they appear after puberty","Six or more; but the macules alone do not establish the diagnosis","Two or more; and the macules alone establish the diagnosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — over five millimetres prepubertal, over fifteen postpubertal. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"You are explaining a 49-year-old woman's melanoma report to her; it gives Breslow thickness, ulceration status and mitotic rate. Which of these does the deck call the dominant prognostic variable?","choices":["None of them; the anatomic site of the primary is the dominant prognostic variable","Breslow thickness, with ulceration and mitotic activity further modifying stage-based prognosis","Ulceration, with Breslow thickness and mitotic activity further modifying prognosis","Mitotic rate, with Breslow thickness and ulceration further modifying prognosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — which is why it must be measured accurately at the initial biopsy. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Conditional probability, as introduced in this lecture, refers to which of the following?","choices":["The probability that a treatment will be tolerated by the patient","The probability that two clinicians reach the same diagnosis","The proportion of a population that will develop a disease over a lifetime","The probability of disease if another event, test result, or condition is present"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines conditional probability as the probability of disease or event given that another event, test result, or condition is present. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which pathway does the MCR-1 receptor act through?","choices":["T-cell mediated cytotoxicity against melanocytes","Cyclic adenosine monophosphate, activating melanogenesis","The insulin-like growth factor 1 receptor pathway","Matrix metalloproteinase activation in the dermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — reduced activity promotes pheomelanin instead. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 52-year-old carpenter has thickened, poorly demarcated, painless skin across both palms. The skin lines run through the thickened area. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Keratoacanthoma","Callus","Hard corn","Verruca vulgaris"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — broad-area pressure, diffuse thickening, no central core, painless. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which variant of granuloma annulare carries the strongest systemic associations?","choices":["Generalised or disseminated, associated with diabetes, thyroid disease, dyslipidaemia and lymphoma","Localised disease, which predominates at about 75% of cases and affects children and young adults","Subcutaneous, occurring almost exclusively in children under six","Perforating, with central umbilication and extrusion of collagen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is more common in adults over forty. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How does vitiligo differ from pityriasis versicolor on the deck's differential?","choices":["Vitiligo has an erythematous yellowish tint with soft greasy scales","Vitiligo begins with a herald patch and spreads in a Christmas tree pattern","Vitiligo shows short hyphae with yeast clusters on microscopy","Vitiligo is completely white — depigmented — and is an autoimmune disorder"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — pityriasis versicolor is hypopigmented rather than depigmented, and it scales. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which lesion is dermatosis papulosa nigrans described as identical to?","choices":["Small seborrheic keratoses","Small solar lentigines","Small blue naevi of the face","Small dysplastic naevi"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the distinction is in the epidemiology and the distribution on face and neck. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What must be protected in Ramsay Hunt syndrome, and why?","choices":["The cornea, if eyelid closure is impaired","The tympanic membrane, if vesicles involve the canal","The inner ear, by avoiding all antivirals","The facial nerve, by avoiding corticosteroids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the facial palsy can prevent the eye from closing. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman treated for scabies three weeks ago still has itchy papules, though no new burrows are seen and her household has been treated. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Oral ivermectin every two weeks for two to three doses alongside topical therapy","Oral antibiotics directed at staphylococcal superinfection of the excoriations","A mid to high potency topical corticosteroid, or intralesional triamcinolone acetonide","A second full course of permethrin applied overnight to the whole skin surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — persistent post-scabietic papules are a recognised complication. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How does the allylamine class of antifungals work, and how do you recognise one by name?","choices":["It blocks synthesis of ergosterol; the names end in -azole","It inhibits mitosis in dividing fungal cells; the names end in -fulvin","It blocks cell wall glucan synthesis; the names end in -fungin","It destroys the fungal cell membrane; the names end in -fine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — terbinafine and naftifine. Destroying the membrane prevents growth and ultimately kills the fungus. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has a firm 8 mm brown nodule on her lower leg. She thinks it appeared after an insect bite two years ago. Squeezing it laterally makes it retract beneath the surface. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Keloid","Epidermoid cyst","Dermatofibroma","Cutaneous melanoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the dimple sign, on the commonest site, after a bite. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"In the FRAMES counselling strategy, what does the letter M represent?","choices":["Motivation of the clinician","Medication review","Monitoring of behaviour change","Menu of options"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. FRAMES is feedback about personal risk, responsibility of the patient, advice to change, menu of options, empathetic style, and promote self-efficacy. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is neurocutaneous melanosis and which patients are at risk?","choices":["A related condition in patients with naevi on the head, neck or posterior midline, causing seizures, hydrocephalus, neurological deficits and vomiting","A related condition in patients with naevi on the trunk and extremities, causing pigmented macules within a tan background patch","A related condition in patients with over one hundred naevi by adolescence, carrying a high risk of progression to melanoma","A related condition in patients with multiple Spitz naevi, associated with an underlying familial cancer syndrome"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the prognosis is poor once neurological symptoms appear, usually in the first few years of life. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has a purulent wound and the clinician wants to know both what is growing and what will treat it. Which test should be ordered?","choices":["Fungal culture of the wound surface","Potassium hydroxide wet preparation","Tzanck smear of the wound base","Bacterial culture and sensitivity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the culture names the organism and the sensitivity directs the antibiotic. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with erysipelas of the leg has a clinically classic presentation. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic approach?","choices":["Blood cultures in every case, since organism identification directs the antibiotic","Computed tomography of the limb to define the depth of tissue involvement","Punch biopsy of the leading edge to separate this from contact dermatitis","No routine testing, since blood and tissue cultures have extremely low yield"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Leukocytosis and raised inflammatory markers are common but not diagnostic. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Which finding in a woman with persistent acne points toward polycystic ovarian syndrome?","choices":["Flushing with hot drinks and visible facial telangiectasias","Pustules pierced by a central hair over the thighs and trunk","Premenstrual flares of acne with an otherwise normal cycle","Hirsutism together with irregular or absent menses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That combination indicates androgen hypersecretion. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the principal risk a patient must understand before endoscopic thoracic sympathectomy?","choices":["Compensatory sweating elsewhere, occurring in over 30%","The need for retreatment every three to six months","Dry mouth and urinary retention as ongoing side effects","Permanent destruction of axillary glands by microwave energy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the procedure is permanent, so the trade-off has to be discussed thoroughly beforehand. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Professor Jaquith treats a small-surface-area non-purulent cellulitis as an outpatient. What follow-up does she insist on, and why?","choices":["Return in one week, once the course is complete","No routine follow-up unless the patient deteriorates","Return in 72 hours for repeat blood cultures","Return in 24 to 48 hours, because it spreads so fast"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — to confirm the antibiotic is working. That interval is the safety net on outpatient treatment. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 1:01:43"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has a classic erythema migrans lesion after a tick bite in Vermont. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Diagnose and treat him on clinical grounds without waiting for serology","Send an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay and withhold treatment until it returns","Send a Western blot and treat only if the immunoglobulin G band is positive","Reassure and review in two weeks, since most tick bites need no treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a patient with the lesion is treated clinically, and testing is most useful outside endemic regions. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman has asymptomatic white non-scaly patches with sharp margins around the mouth, on the fingertips and in the genital area, appearing symmetrically over six months. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea versicolor of the face and trunk","Post-inflammatory hypopigmentation","Non-segmental vitiligo","Segmental vitiligo"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — symmetrical distribution with a preference for face, genitals and acral areas. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 30-year-old Black patient asks whether she needs to worry about melanoma. Which is the most accurate response using the deck's figures?","choices":["Lifetime risk is effectively zero in persons of colour, so routine skin checks are unnecessary","Lifetime risk is the same in all skin tones, at about 2%","Lifetime risk is higher in persons of colour, at about 2% against 0.1 to 0.5% in white individuals","Lifetime risk is lower but not zero — about 0.1 to 0.5% in persons of colour against about 2% in white individuals"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — lower risk, and acral and nail sites still need checking. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A patient with tinea incognito has extensive, follicular disease. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Systemic antifungal therapy","Topical antifungal therapy alone","A more potent topical corticosteroid","Oral antibiotic therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — extensive, follicular or refractory infection warrants systemic therapy. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A test is described as able to correctly identify patients who do NOT have the disease. Which property is being described?","choices":["Pretest probability","Sensitivity","Specificity","Posttest probability"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture's paired prompt defines specificity as the ability to correctly identify patients who do not have the disease or condition. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"When should cultures be obtained in impetigo?","choices":["Only in children under three years of age, since they are the group at highest risk of complications","When the patient is at high risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, or when post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is present","In every case at first presentation, since staphylococcal and streptococcal disease are treated differently from one another","Only when the lesions have failed to respond after a full two-week course of topical mupirocin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a health-care worker or teacher is the example given of high risk. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 93"}]};
